# Firmware SEND audit

Implementation of record: archived Incident B
[source](../../../../artifacts/arq/20261113T123620Z-reverse-raw/source/src/reverse_raw/main.cpp).
Current equivalent line references follow; its hash is recorded in that bundle.
Active ARQ is separately `src/arq/main.cpp`, `sender.c`, `receiver.c`, `codec.c`.
Original raw MVP is `src/mvp/main.cpp`. No protocol code was changed here.

| Transition | Source/function | Preconditions / mutation / observable failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Host SEND | tools/arq_trial.py Capture.command:178 | Live process, complete pipe write/flush; records host timestamp; does not confirm MCU delivery |
| Host byte to mailbox | third_party/upditerm/upditerm.py SerialUPDI.send1:243 | Read GPIOR2; wait FLAG_RX clear; write GPIOR3 then set FLAG_RX; strict ACKs |
| Mailbox consumption | third_party/upditerm/updi_uart.c updi_uart_rx:60 | Poll FLAG_RX; read byte then clear flag; loop checks poll before blocking accessor |
| Byte parser | src/reverse_raw/main.cpp command_byte:73 | Ignore CR; collect at most47 bytes; newline invokes command; overflow rejects whole line then resets parser |
| Gate/payload | command:54 | STATUS/X special; ready, not active, not used; exact ASCII command with22 hex digits required |
| Pre-TX snapshot | configuration:19, command:60 | Reserve logger slot, read23 SPI registers, enqueue; no connected reader wait |
| Start radio | command:61 → SX127x::startTransmit:571 | used=true; exact static11 bytes; rc sets active; started set on return |
| Start log | command:62–64 | RAW_TX after driver return, then RAW_TX_STATE; rc error cleans up; absence does not locate execution inside preceding call |
| Completion | loop:102–106 | Poll IRQ; TxDone or3000ms bound → finishTransmit → event → standby |

No RAW_TX_PRE in B means its pre-snapshot was not delivered to the host. It
does not prove command parsing never began: enqueue is not delivery. No RAW_TX
also does not prove startTransmit was never called; logging follows its return.

Logging uses16 slots of192 bytes and emits at most one mailbox character per
loop when the reader is ready (`src/arq/diagnostics.h`). Full logger increments
overflow and returns immediately. Buffer backpressure cannot block the loop by
design; SPI/RadioLib calls still take bounded execution time and must not be
confused with waiting for host logging. No command-state ISR appears in this
path. The host reader/writer serial lock prevents concurrent UPDI transactions;
no reproducible logging/parser collision has been demonstrated.

Host-only `tests/rca_send_test.cpp` exercises actual parser/loop with existing
fake radio: partial command does not transmit; CR/LF completes exact11 bytes;
wrong length and overfull input reject; repeated command starts only once;
disconnected/full logger does not prevent TX timeout cleanup. These results
are simulated execution, not evidence that Incident B's SEND reached its MCU.
Existing reverse/ARQ tests cover CRC, pending packet at deadline, RX-arm rejection
without IRQ clearing, malformed lengths, and cleanup after transport failure.

Reset evidence limitation: installed DxCore
`cores/dxcore/main.cpp:init_reset_flags` lines144–151 reads RSTFR, clears observed
bits and stores GPIOR0; flags0 requests a software reset. Normal ARQ setup copies
GPR_GPR0 into BOOT diagnostics. Reverse setup does not log that value or reset
generation. Uptime/build/status are available, but no continuous reset record
survives this capture gap. Later programming resets cannot diagnose the earlier
SEND. A proposed reset logger must account for the core's existing early clear;
simply adding a late RSTFR read could record zero incorrectly. No such edit made.
</file>

<file path="docs/arq/investigations/rca-20261113/GATE1_SOURCE_AUDIT.md">
# Gate 1 source audit

The active ARQ V1 source and evidence repository is `SENIOR_PROJECT_RCA`. The
user-supplied `gateway-lora/loraboard` directory is a separate legacy component
under the `iwing-tracker` Git root; it does not contain the specified ARQ
runner, UPDI Monitor or investigation files. No files in that legacy worktree
were changed.

## Normal UPDI Monitor path

In `third_party/upditerm/upditerm.py`, `UPDI.__init__` at172–183 sends exactly
two `00` BREAK characters at300 baud, restores38400 baud, and sends a SYNCH
framed STCS to disable contention detection. It does not call `reset()`. The
normal Monitor initializes SerialUPDI at230–236: OCD key followed by volatile
UART enable. The optional reset parameter defaults false and the reset call is
conditional. `Terminal` at314–396 serializes its reader/writer traffic.
`SerialUPDI.recv1` at252–263 resumes a STOPPED CPU through OCD_RUN; this is a
resume action, not a reset. Gate 1 records any occurrence. Normal cleanup
disables the mailbox and UPDI interface. No BREAK/recovery loop is used.

For identity, the same UPDI link reads the32-byte SIB, then identifies the
exact part from the raw signature-row bytes (the SIB product field is compact
and does not spell the full `AVR128DA28` model). It also reads the unique
serial directly from the read-only signature row. Microchip DS40002183C (2021), printed pp39–43, maps
SIGROW at data address0x1100, signature bytes at offsets00–02 and unique
serial bytes at offsets10–1f. AVR128DA28 signature is `1E 97 0A`.
The LDS instruction uses SizeA=01 for a16-bit address, SizeB=00 for one-byte
data, opcode `04`; address bytes are low then high. Gate 1 reads addresses
0x1100–0x1102 and0x1110–0x111f over the captured UPDI transport. These are
read-only accesses; no NVM programming key or data write is involved.

The normal monitor then uses the same OCD and SRAM-mailbox operations as the
existing SerialUPDI class. Status requests access GPIOR2 at0x1e and GPIOR3 at
0x1f. Firmware `status()` in `src/arq/main.cpp:79–99` reports readiness,
overflow, session, counters and register snapshot. `command()` at126–128 routes
STATUS to that handler; it does not initiate TX or RX. The status loop may
continue unrelated existing firmware work, so successful complete status and
idle session are checked each time.

## Reset-capable identity alternative excluded

The installed AVRDUDE is8.1. Its normal SerialUPDI initialization enters NVM
programming. The tagged source `serialupdi.c` calls `serialupdi_enter_progmode`
and toggles APPLY_RESET/RELEASE_RESET. That optional identity method was not
used because Monitor acquisition does not require reset. Primary source:
[AVRDUDE v8.1 SerialUPDI source](https://github.com/avrdudes/avrdude/blob/v8.1/src/serialupdi.c#L2719-L2865)
and [initialization](https://github.com/avrdudes/avrdude/blob/v8.1/src/serialupdi.c#L3140-L3298).

## Target and application identity

`config/current_hardware.json` maps Tracker's receiver role to
`/dev/serial/by-path/pci-0000:00:14.0-usb-0:1:1.0-port0`, the configured Tracker
MCU serial and expected fuse baseline. Before opening the port, Gate 1 checks
that stable path resolves, udev reports CP2102 VID10c4/PIDea60 at USB path
`pci-0000:00:14.0-usb-0:1:1.0`, `fuser` reports no owner, and serial opening
obtains the exclusive OS lock. Live SIB, signature and unique serial are then
compared to AVR128DA28 and current hardware configuration. Startup firmware
build/config and receiver role are compared to the last verified Tracker
standby image and generated ARQ manifest. A mismatch stops before STATUS. This
firmware may not emit a new unsolicited startup block when an already-running
application has seen a previous attach/detach. The first explicit STATUS request
therefore establishes the command path and verifies firmware identity; it is
transaction1 of the 20-request maximum, not an extra uncounted probe.

## Historical incident boundary

Incident A `20261113T121506Z` remains PHYSICAL_RF_FAILURE_UNISOLATED. Incident B
`20261113T123620Z` remains HOST_TRANSPORT_ERROR / RF INCONCLUSIVE. A later
monitor disconnect is not treated as proof of reset or USB removal. The blocked
`20261113T142138Z` Gate 1 attempt had0 STATUS requests. Its two-break conflict
was resolved by this task's explicit authorization; it is not hardware evidence.
</file>

